{0 S3 Guides}

Guides for the redesigned S3 API. The public surface is streaming-first,
AWS-provider-aware, and result-returning.

{1 Object Operations}

Primitive object operations live under [Object]. They use runtime-owned upload
bodies and scoped download readers.

{2 Upload}

{[
let body = Awskit_s3_eio.Runtime.string_body "hello" in
Awskit_s3_eio.Object.put s3
  ~bucket:"my-bucket"
  ~key:"hello.txt"
  ~body
  ()
]}

Primitive uploads require a known content length. The runtime records length
and payload hash in its upload-body descriptor before signing.

Options carry object metadata, content type, storage class, tags,
preconditions, checksum headers, and server-side encryption headers:

{[
let options =
  {
    Awskit_s3.Object.Put.default_options with
    content_type = Some "text/plain";
    metadata = [ ("origin", "example") ];
    tags = [ { Awskit_s3.Tag.key = "env"; value = "dev" } ];
  }
in
Awskit_s3_eio.Object.put s3
  ~bucket:"my-bucket"
  ~key:"hello.txt"
  ~options
  ~body:(Awskit_s3_eio.Runtime.string_body "hello")
  ()
]}

{2 Download}

[Object.get] scopes the response body to the [consume] callback. Do not store
the reader outside that callback.

{[
let consume reader =
  let bytes = Bytes.create 8192 in
  match Awskit_s3_eio.Runtime.read reader bytes ~off:0 ~len:8192 with
  | Error _ as error -> error
  | Ok n -> Ok (Bytes.sub_string bytes 0 n)
in
Awskit_s3_eio.Object.get s3
  ~bucket:"my-bucket"
  ~key:"large.bin"
  ~consume
  ()
]}

Use [Object.head] when only metadata is needed. Use [Object.exists] for boolean
existence checks. Missing-object and missing-bucket behavior is reported as
structured service errors by primitive operations.

{2 Copy, Delete, List, Tagging}

The object surface also includes:

{ul
  {li [Object.delete] and [Object.delete_many]}
  {li [Object.copy]}
  {li [Object.list] for rich pages and [Object.list_keys] for key-only pages}
  {li [Object.Tagging.get], [put], and [delete]}
}

{1 Object.Buffer}

[Object.Buffer] is for explicit in-memory use. Upload helpers accept strings or
bytes. Download helpers require [max_size] to avoid accidental unbounded
buffering.

{[
Awskit_s3_eio.Object.Buffer.put_string s3
  ~bucket:"my-bucket"
  ~key:"hello.txt"
  "hello"
]}

{[
Awskit_s3_eio.Object.Buffer.get_string s3
  ~bucket:"my-bucket"
  ~key:"hello.txt"
  ~max_size:1_048_576L
  ()
]}

If the response body exceeds [max_size], the helper returns
[Error (Awskit.Error.Body _)].

{1 Object.Transfer}

Unix-capable adapters expose local-path helpers under [Object.Transfer].
These helpers are adapter-level APIs because pure [awskit-s3] must not depend
on filesystem APIs.

{2 Eio}

{[
Awskit_s3_eio.Object.Transfer.upload_from_path s3
  ~bucket:"my-bucket"
  ~key:"archive.tar"
  ~path
  ()
]}

{[
Awskit_s3_eio.Object.Transfer.download_to_path s3
  ~bucket:"my-bucket"
  ~key:"archive.tar"
  ~path
  ()
]}

{2 Lwt Unix}

{[
let* result =
  Awskit_s3_lwt_unix.Object.Transfer.upload_from_path s3
    ~bucket:"my-bucket"
    ~key:"archive.tar"
    ~path:"/tmp/archive.tar"
    ()
]}

Current transfer helpers are convenience helpers. Managed concurrent multipart
transfer, progress callbacks, resumability, and runtime-native file streaming
are future work.

{1 AWS S3 Provider Configuration}

S3 provider configuration is AWS-specific. Pass [provider] when constructing an
adapter connection or using standalone presigned helpers.

{[
let provider =
  Awskit_s3.Provider.aws
    ~endpoint_variant:`Dualstack
    ~addressing_style:`Auto
    ()
in
let s3 =
  Awskit_s3_eio.create ~sw ~env ~region ~credentials ~provider ()
]}

Endpoint variants:

{ul
  {li [`Regional] — [s3.<region>.amazonaws.com]}
  {li [`Dualstack] — [s3.dualstack.<region>.amazonaws.com]}
  {li [`Fips] — [s3-fips.<region>.amazonaws.com]}
  {li [`Fips_dualstack] — [s3-fips.dualstack.<region>.amazonaws.com]}
  {li [`Accelerate] — [s3-accelerate.amazonaws.com]}
  {li [`Accelerate_dualstack] — [s3-accelerate.dualstack.amazonaws.com]}
}

Addressing styles:

{ul
  {li [`Virtual_hosted] puts the bucket in the host name.}
  {li [`Path] puts the bucket in the path.}
  {li [`Auto] uses virtual-hosted style when valid and falls back to path-style
    for dotted bucket names over HTTPS.}
}

Transfer Acceleration requires virtual-hosted-style-compatible bucket names and
must be enabled on the bucket in AWS.

{1 Bucket Operations}

[Bucket] covers general-purpose bucket basics and the first configuration
subresources:

{ul
  {li [Bucket.create], [delete], [head], [exists], [list], [get_location]}
  {li [Bucket.Policy.get], [put], [delete]}
  {li [Bucket.Policy_status.get]}
  {li [Bucket.Versioning.get], [put]}
  {li [Bucket.Tagging.get], [put], [delete]}
  {li [Bucket.Encryption.get], [put], [delete]}
  {li [Bucket.Cors.get], [put], [delete]}
  {li [Bucket.Website.get], [put], [delete] for index/error documents}
  {li [Bucket.Public_access_block.get], [put], [delete]}
  {li [Bucket.Ownership_controls.get], [put], [delete]}
  {li [Bucket.Request_payment.get], [put]}
  {li [Bucket.Accelerate.get], [put]}
  {li [Bucket.Logging.get], [put] for target bucket/prefix logging}
}

[Bucket.Policy] sends and receives validated JSON policy payloads. It does not
construct IAM policies or evaluate policy authorization decisions.

{[
let encryption =
  {
    Awskit_s3.Bucket.Encryption.rules =
      [
        {
          Awskit_s3.Bucket.Encryption.Rule.sse_algorithm =
            Awskit_s3.Bucket.Encryption.Algorithm.Aes256;
          kms_master_key_id = None;
        };
      ];
  }
in
Awskit_s3_eio.Bucket.Encryption.put s3
  ~bucket:"my-bucket"
  encryption
]}

{1 Multipart Uploads}

[Multipart] exposes the primitive AWS multipart flow:

{ol
  {li [Multipart.create] starts an upload and returns an upload ID.}
  {li [Multipart.upload_part] uploads a known-length part.}
  {li [Multipart.complete] completes the upload with ordered parts.}
  {li [Multipart.abort] cancels an upload.}
  {li [Multipart.list_parts] inspects uploaded parts.}
}

Managed high-level multipart transfer is not implemented yet.

{1 Presigned URLs}

Use adapter-level [Presigned] modules when a connection already exists, or use
standalone {!module:Awskit_s3.Presigned} helpers when generating URLs without
an adapter connection.

{[
Awskit_s3_eio.Presigned.get_object s3
  ~bucket:"my-bucket"
  ~key:"public.txt"
  ()
]}

{[
Awskit_s3.Presigned.put_object
  ~region
  ~credentials
  ~now
  ~provider
  ~bucket:"my-bucket"
  ~key:"upload.txt"
  ()
]}

Presigned URLs use [UNSIGNED-PAYLOAD] and the same provider endpoint resolver
as normal operations.

{1 Credentials}

Eio S3 connections require explicit [region] and [credentials].

{[
let credentials =
  Awskit.Credentials.create_exn
    ~access_key_id:"AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE"
    ~secret_access_key:"wJalrXUtnFEMI/K7MDENG/bPxRfiCYEXAMPLEKEY"
    ()
in
let region = Awskit.Region.of_string_exn "us-east-1" in
Awskit_s3_eio.create ~sw ~env ~region ~credentials ()
]}

[awskit-s3.lwt_unix] can use explicit values or load the standard environment
variables through [awskit.lwt_unix]:

{[
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY
AWS_SESSION_TOKEN
AWS_REGION
AWS_DEFAULT_REGION
]}

{[
Awskit_s3_lwt_unix.create ()
]}

The current credential helpers cover static credentials and environment
variables. Shared AWS config files, named profiles, ECS/EC2 metadata, web
identity, STS assume-role, and refreshable credentials remain future work.

{1 Errors}

S3 operations return [(value, Awskit_s3.Error.t) result] or that result inside
the selected runtime monad.

[Awskit_s3.Error.t] is {!type:Awskit.Error.t}. It preserves validation,
signing, transport, service, decode, and body-consumption failures.

Use S3 classifiers for service-specific checks:

{[
match result with
| Ok value -> use value
| Error err when Awskit_s3.Error.is_no_such_key err -> handle_missing ()
| Error err when Awskit_s3.Error.is_precondition_failed err -> retry_later ()
| Error err -> log_error err
]}

For service failures, [Awskit_s3.Error.service_code] exposes the AWS error
code when present.

{1 Simulator Testing}

{!module:Awskit_s3.Sim} exposes the same synchronous S3 operation shape as the
runtime-backed client and adds test-only helpers:

{ul
  {li deterministic clock}
  {li in-memory store inspection}
  {li operation history}
  {li deterministic fault injection}
}

{[
let clock = Awskit_s3.Sim.Clock.create () in
let store = Awskit_s3.Sim.create_store ~clock () in
let conn = Awskit_s3.Sim.connect store ~credentials in

ignore
  (Awskit_s3.Sim.Bucket.create conn ~bucket:"test-bucket" ()
   |> Result.get_ok);

ignore
  (Awskit_s3.Sim.Object.Buffer.put_string conn
     ~bucket:"test-bucket"
     ~key:"hello.txt"
     "hello"
   |> Result.get_ok)
]}

[Sim.Response_lost] is modeled as a body-read failure. [Sim.Slow_down],
[Sim.Internal_error], and [Sim.Connection_reset] fail object operations before
mutation. Multipart simulator semantics and object version history are still
explicitly deferred.
